  "final_text": "All four steps are done, and the read-back shows the rule enabled.\n\n1. **Data view:** \"Kibana Sample Data Flights\" (id `d3d7af60-4c81-11e8-b3d7-01146121b73d`, index `kibana_sample_data_flights`) has 33 fields. Its time field is `timestamp`.\n2. **Dashboard:** \"S4-44b0aa77 Flights\" (id `9d026a84-30e0-4e1c-9830-85b2b5a4d717`) has exactly two panels, both on that data view:\n   - A line chart of flight count over time, by `timestamp`.\n   - A bar chart of flight count by `Carrier`, showing the top 10 carriers.\n3. **Rule:** \"S4-44b0aa77 Cancelled flights\" (id `f4dc19f0-2cab-4862-93a2-7840a9b14df9`) is an Elasticsearch query rule. It runs every minute and fires when more than 10 documents with `Cancelled: true` arrive in a 5-minute window. I created it with `enabled: true`, so I didn't need to enable it afterwards. It has no actions, so when it fires it only creates an alert.\n4. **Edit and read-back:** I renamed the carrier panel from \"Flights per carrier\" to \"Flights by carrier\", and a fresh read of the dashboard shows the new title. Reading the rule back shows `enabled: true`, a 1m schedule and last execution status `ok`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
